1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,700
Now, for you, Scoutmasters.

2
00:00:08,900 --> 00:00:12,140
Hey, this is Clark Green. Welcome to the Scoutmasters podcast.

3
00:00:13,180 --> 00:00:18,300
So, I made an interesting historical discovery I wanted to share with you.

4
00:00:18,440 --> 00:00:25,180
Plato. Plato, the Greek philosopher from, you know, what, 400 BC.

5
00:00:25,180 --> 00:00:30,100
Plato apparently was a Scoutmaster. Yep.

6
00:00:30,860 --> 00:00:36,500
I read this quote. He said, of all the animals, the boy is the most unmanageable.

7
00:00:38,140 --> 00:00:41,400
What do you think? I think he probably was in scouting, don't you?

8
00:00:42,200 --> 00:00:44,820
Hey, this is podcast number five.

9
00:00:55,180 --> 00:01:19,500
We've got a pretty full slate for podcast number five.

10
00:01:19,500 --> 00:01:26,340
We're going to start with Scoutmastership in seven minutes, talking about transitioning Weebelo's den leaders into a Scout troop.

11
00:01:26,460 --> 00:01:28,740
That can be a bit of a trick. It was for me.

12
00:01:29,480 --> 00:01:34,700
Then we're going to, I've got a little story about a young Scoutmaster's first hike with his troop.

13
00:01:35,320 --> 00:01:41,640
And that will be followed by our fifth installment of instructional methods for Scouts.

14
00:01:42,600 --> 00:01:45,820
And a Scoutmasters minute about Founders Day.

15
00:01:45,820 --> 00:01:49,000
If you don't know about Founders Day, listen to the Scoutmasters minute.

16
00:01:49,580 --> 00:01:50,800
It'll be worth your time.

17
00:01:51,360 --> 00:01:53,039
I want to tell you about Ask Andy.

18
00:01:53,259 --> 00:01:59,039
If you haven't already discovered it, Ask Andy is read probably by thousands of scouters every single time he posts a new column.

19
00:01:59,539 --> 00:02:04,220
He's at the U.S. Scouting Service Project, and I'll have a link to Ask Andy on the blog.

20
00:02:04,220 --> 00:02:16,740
Andy is a commissioner of some years and experience who has been a Scout and an Eagle Scout and really has a good handle on Scouting and is well worth reading.

21
00:02:17,360 --> 00:02:18,420
He's very plain spoken.

22
00:02:18,600 --> 00:02:19,600
He doesn't waste your time.

23
00:02:20,200 --> 00:02:30,660
And he fields questions from all over 50 states and 300 and some councils and puts very good answers in his column.

24
00:02:30,820 --> 00:02:31,740
So do go check it out.

25
00:02:32,000 --> 00:02:33,420
Hey, let's get ourselves started.

26
00:02:33,420 --> 00:02:38,000
Scoutmastership in seven minutes or less.

27
00:02:46,440 --> 00:02:50,660
So I was Weevilos Den Leader for 18 Weevilos.

28
00:02:51,679 --> 00:02:52,519
Yeah, 18.

29
00:02:53,160 --> 00:02:54,160
Eight-teen.

30
00:02:54,420 --> 00:02:55,179
Count them, 18.

31
00:02:55,500 --> 00:02:56,859
I mean, we had a sideshow going.

32
00:02:57,519 --> 00:03:01,579
You know, a whip and a chair and a sidearm.

33
00:03:02,340 --> 00:03:04,680
That's what you needed to run that den every once in a while.

34
00:03:05,420 --> 00:03:06,260
They were great guys.

35
00:03:06,380 --> 00:03:06,880
Great guys.

36
00:03:07,180 --> 00:03:11,120
So we transitioned into the Scout Troop, and they needed a new Scoutmaster.

37
00:03:11,520 --> 00:03:14,580
So I went from being a Weevilos Den Leader to being a Scoutmaster.

38
00:03:14,680 --> 00:03:15,900
And that is quite a transition.

39
00:03:16,940 --> 00:03:21,700
Working with Weevilos is really, it's fundamentally different than working with Scouts.

40
00:03:21,700 --> 00:03:30,700
As a Weevilos Den Leader, I mean, you're required to do most of the planning and the leading that are going to be the responsibility of youth leadership in a Scout Troop.

41
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:39,480
But it's a pretty intense job being a Weevilos Den Leader in comparison to being a Scoutmaster or Assistant Scoutmaster.

42
00:03:40,420 --> 00:03:47,220
Making the shift from Den Leader to Assistant Scoutmaster or Scoutmaster in a Scout Troop is pretty important stuff.

43
00:03:47,739 --> 00:03:48,540
And it's not easy.

44
00:03:49,040 --> 00:03:49,700
It's not easy.

45
00:03:49,700 --> 00:03:53,440
Because I don't care how smart you are or how much training you get.

46
00:03:53,839 --> 00:03:57,640
You have been conditioned to think one way about the boys that were in your den.

47
00:03:57,799 --> 00:04:00,799
And it's not going to be easy to change that.

48
00:04:01,700 --> 00:04:04,560
Den leaders are stepping into a totally new role.

49
00:04:05,119 --> 00:04:07,640
And they need some transition help.

50
00:04:07,640 --> 00:04:18,899
Now, normally, you know, absolutely they need to take advantage of whatever training is offered, whatever training courses are going to be offered, you know, Scoutmaster fundamentals and all of that.

51
00:04:19,479 --> 00:04:34,120
But beyond that, they're going to need to have a little sit down with the Scoutmaster and some of the other adult leaders in the troop so that you can discuss some of the things that you know are going to happen and what their appropriate reactions are going to be.

52
00:04:34,120 --> 00:04:53,080
So, you know, one of the things that is absolutely going to happen is the Scouts that were in the den are going to come and ask their den leader, their former den leader, questions about what's happening next and how do I do this and when are we going to go here and what do I need to do?

53
00:04:53,560 --> 00:05:03,700
Well, what has to happen then is that former den leader, new Assistant Scoutmaster, needs to know that he sends any question where?

54
00:05:04,600 --> 00:05:07,720
That's right, that's right, to the boy's patrol leader.

55
00:05:08,540 --> 00:05:18,280
Whether he knows, whether the former den leader knows the answer or not, he needs to not answer questions for his boys.

56
00:05:18,280 --> 00:05:19,820
That's hard.

57
00:05:19,979 --> 00:05:21,080
That's really hard.

58
00:05:21,280 --> 00:05:35,219
And it's hard to see why sometimes, but you have to cement the idea that that patrol leader is the guy who is responsible now for knowing stuff and for that patrol functioning.

59
00:05:35,539 --> 00:05:38,020
And it's not the den leader's responsibility anymore.

60
00:05:38,020 --> 00:05:42,260
There are going to be concerns.

61
00:05:42,260 --> 00:05:57,520
The den leader's going to watch his former Weeblos in that new patrol, and they're going to flounder a little bit, and the patrol leader is not going to be as savvy as he was as a leader probably, and he's going to have some concerns.

62
00:05:57,800 --> 00:05:58,900
He's going to feel uncomfortable.

63
00:05:59,400 --> 00:06:01,080
He's going to feel a little antsy about it.

64
00:06:01,320 --> 00:06:02,420
But we know that, right?

65
00:06:02,880 --> 00:06:03,599
So we tell him.

66
00:06:03,860 --> 00:06:04,560
We tell him that.

67
00:06:04,740 --> 00:06:07,740
We say, hey, you know, you're going to feel antsy and uncomfortable.

68
00:06:07,740 --> 00:06:21,920
It's not easy to watch, but you're not going to have a whole lot of confidence in some of these youth leaders every once in a while, and we just want to let you know that that is basically the way we feel, too.

69
00:06:23,320 --> 00:06:24,500
They're developing leaders.

70
00:06:24,640 --> 00:06:26,780
They're not going to be as sharp as you were.

71
00:06:27,220 --> 00:06:35,940
They're not sharp as we are, but they get to do the same thing that we got to do, and that is develop without a whole lot of interference

72
00:06:35,940 --> 00:06:38,440
and without a whole lot of second guessing.

73
00:06:39,219 --> 00:06:46,719
So if you have a big concern about, or a small concern, about the way things are being done and what youth leaders are doing, don't go to them.

74
00:06:47,660 --> 00:06:48,440
Don't go to them.

75
00:06:48,600 --> 00:06:58,040
Come to either the Scoutmaster or one of your fellow assistants and, you know, kind of vent, and we'll try and help you shoulder the burden.

76
00:06:59,320 --> 00:07:00,940
Because, you know, we all do it, too.

77
00:07:00,940 --> 00:07:05,500
So it can be a very trying time.

78
00:07:05,880 --> 00:07:14,320
The other thing to understand is that if you, as a Scout leader, go to the Senior Patrol leader or patrol leader or any one of the youth leaders in the troop,

79
00:07:14,380 --> 00:07:17,180
and you start expressing concerns, they're going to take it as direction.

80
00:07:17,659 --> 00:07:28,200
They're very polite young men, and they're very sensitive to taking direction and being loyal and obedient and respectful of adults.

81
00:07:28,200 --> 00:07:35,920
So you have to be very cautious with the way that you approach them and what you say and how you express your concerns.

82
00:07:36,320 --> 00:07:37,700
You're going to learn how to do that.

83
00:07:38,120 --> 00:07:39,460
You're going to learn when to do that.

84
00:07:40,480 --> 00:07:41,860
And we're going to teach you.

85
00:07:42,420 --> 00:07:48,620
So if you have concerns about the way things are going or you have a question about the way things are going,

86
00:07:48,980 --> 00:07:52,599
probably the best thing to do is to turn to one of your fellow adult leaders and ask them

87
00:07:52,600 --> 00:07:57,120
and kind of leave the boys out of it for now until you kind of get the hang of things.

88
00:07:58,360 --> 00:08:06,620
So let's say we're in the middle of a troop meeting or camp out and you see something that is an immediate danger to safety or propriety.

89
00:08:07,160 --> 00:08:08,180
You can stop it.

90
00:08:08,540 --> 00:08:12,020
You can step in as an adult and make it stop.

91
00:08:12,020 --> 00:08:16,419
And that's fine, you know, because we do have to occasionally do that.

92
00:08:16,760 --> 00:08:17,780
It's not once a week.

93
00:08:18,180 --> 00:08:19,200
It's not once a month.

94
00:08:19,440 --> 00:08:27,200
It's not once every six months that we have to step in and put the brakes on the program that the scouts are running.

95
00:08:27,280 --> 00:08:28,039
It's pretty rare.

96
00:08:28,359 --> 00:08:30,460
So don't be looking for that opportunity.

97
00:08:30,740 --> 00:08:38,659
But understand if something is going bad, yeah, you have the authority and the responsibility to stop, to make it stop.

98
00:08:38,659 --> 00:08:48,000
So that's just, you know, I'm just trying to paint an idea of the way this discussion would go for a new assistant scoutmaster or scoutmaster

99
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:52,800
and just some of the key points that they need to know.

100
00:08:53,439 --> 00:09:02,880
And I think it takes somewhere between four and six months before they get anywhere near comfortable with the way things are going on.

101
00:09:02,880 --> 00:09:11,920
And I have tried both ways of just kind of throwing them in the deep end and seeing how they react and doing a little bit of orientation.

102
00:09:12,100 --> 00:09:14,540
And I think a little bit of orientation is real important.

103
00:09:15,040 --> 00:09:25,380
So that's just a few thoughts on how to handle the transition of a Weeblos den leader becoming a Boy Scout leader in a Boy Scout troop.

104
00:09:25,380 --> 00:09:54,520
This has to be the truth, folks, because there is no way.

105
00:09:55,380 --> 00:09:58,000
Anyone could make this up.

106
00:10:00,600 --> 00:10:03,620
I'd like to introduce you to Rice Cochran.

107
00:10:04,600 --> 00:10:11,780
Rice E. Cochran, an author who wrote a book called Be Prepared in 1952.

108
00:10:12,680 --> 00:10:16,540
The Life and Illusions of a Scoutmaster is subtitled.

109
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:23,520
And his book went on to be the basis for the movie Mr. Scoutmaster, if you've ever heard of that.

110
00:10:23,520 --> 00:10:28,300
But, you know, it's a Hollywood look at scouting.

111
00:10:28,680 --> 00:10:34,199
But the book is, I think, a lot funnier and a lot more interesting.

112
00:10:35,760 --> 00:10:41,760
Here's a description as a young scoutmaster of one of the first hikes he took his scout troop on.

113
00:10:41,760 --> 00:10:50,200
They've been hiking around for a while, and he says this,

114
00:10:50,200 --> 00:11:19,480
At last, a keen-eyed lookout shouted,

115
00:11:19,480 --> 00:11:19,820
Water!

116
00:11:20,060 --> 00:11:21,300
Drinking water ahead!

117
00:11:22,960 --> 00:11:29,440
Now that we had all been snatched from the jaws of death, the troop felt rejuvenated as well as redeemed, and evidently forgave me.

118
00:11:30,160 --> 00:11:37,660
By elaborate non-surprise when the water faucet was found, I contrived to give the impression that I had known about it all along,

119
00:11:37,660 --> 00:11:40,819
and perhaps even arranged for it in my own inscrutable fashion.

120
00:11:40,880 --> 00:11:44,740
And I was like, oh, my God!

121
00:11:44,740 --> 00:11:50,440
There seemed to be a tacit assumption that I was more or less vindicated.

122
00:11:51,140 --> 00:11:53,800
That forgiveness gradually became evident in an odd manner.

123
00:11:54,640 --> 00:11:58,300
I realized during the subsequent hour that I was truly slowing down.

124
00:11:58,400 --> 00:12:00,920
My feet seemed heavier, and so did my knapsack.

125
00:12:01,640 --> 00:12:04,920
The uphill stretches felt steeper than they looked, and for a while,

126
00:12:04,920 --> 00:12:10,620
I ascribed this to the onset of middle age, but finally I began to be somewhat alarmed.

127
00:12:11,339 --> 00:12:18,920
I had never before gotten tired so quickly, except in high-altitude mountain hiking with a full overnight pack on my back.

128
00:12:19,880 --> 00:12:20,880
My pack.

129
00:12:20,880 --> 00:12:24,720
Suddenly, I knew what was wrong.

130
00:12:31,020 --> 00:12:35,100
The troop exploded with long-suppressed laughter as I took off the knapsack,

131
00:12:35,580 --> 00:12:38,640
opened it, and dug down until I verified my suspicion.

132
00:12:39,560 --> 00:12:44,980
There were rocks in it, evidently placed there during our stop at the water faucet.

133
00:12:45,700 --> 00:12:50,420
I grinned as bravely through the tempest of mirth as I could and lightened my pack.

134
00:12:50,880 --> 00:12:55,200
I sensed the rocks were the troop's way of showing me that I was back in good standing.

135
00:12:58,100 --> 00:13:01,740
Oh, rocks in the backpack. Yes, a classic.

136
00:13:20,880 --> 00:13:33,820
There's a quartermaster everybody knows.

137
00:13:34,080 --> 00:13:37,140
There's a song about him, this is how it goes.

138
00:13:37,480 --> 00:13:40,380
Just a little ditty doesn't mean a thing.

139
00:13:40,720 --> 00:13:43,760
But when the boys are marching, how they love to sing.

140
00:13:43,760 --> 00:13:50,000
There were mice, mice, eating up the rice in the stores, in the stores.

141
00:13:50,740 --> 00:13:57,000
There were rats, rats, big as blooming cats in the quartermaster's stores.

142
00:13:57,819 --> 00:14:01,380
My eyes are dim, I cannot see.

143
00:14:01,380 --> 00:14:05,100
I have not brought my specs with me.

144
00:14:05,100 --> 00:14:12,520
I have not brought my specs with me.

145
00:14:16,600 --> 00:14:22,240
So this is our last installment, number five, of Instructional Methods for Scouts.

146
00:14:22,240 --> 00:14:37,000
We may return to the subject later, but this is a good basis of information on the way that Scout instruction is different from a lot of other teaching situations.

147
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:45,480
And some interesting methods and promising practices that I've used over time that I think you will find useful.

148
00:14:45,760 --> 00:14:59,700
Now you can find all of this information also at the blog, at the Scoutmaster blog, at scoutmaster.typepad.com.

149
00:14:59,700 --> 00:15:07,500
You know, naturally, it goes without saying, but I'm going to say it anyway, that good instructions based on good preparation.

150
00:15:08,380 --> 00:15:14,000
And that instructors should be able to begin with a clear goal and a real good understanding of their subject.

151
00:15:14,400 --> 00:15:19,160
And then resolve to keep the session active, focused, and brief.

152
00:15:19,540 --> 00:15:23,020
As brief, you know, only as long as necessary.

153
00:15:23,740 --> 00:15:26,860
An instructor with a plan can always adjust.

154
00:15:26,860 --> 00:15:30,360
Okay, because things will change during the session.

155
00:15:30,700 --> 00:15:34,000
A scout may ask questions or a new thought may come up.

156
00:15:34,120 --> 00:15:40,880
But without a plan, we're likely to digress and drift and yield to distractions and generally lose our way.

157
00:15:41,180 --> 00:15:45,580
And I am a great one for digressing and drifting and yielding.

158
00:15:45,780 --> 00:15:50,340
And, you know, if you start talking about something, then another idea comes up in your mind.

159
00:15:50,340 --> 00:15:55,380
And pretty soon, you're talking about summer camp 23 years ago and something that happened.

160
00:15:55,380 --> 00:15:58,120
And then that guy, you know how it is?

161
00:15:58,260 --> 00:15:59,500
That's called a digression.

162
00:16:00,020 --> 00:16:01,140
Stay away from that.

163
00:16:01,540 --> 00:16:02,460
Stick with your plan.

164
00:16:02,960 --> 00:16:04,540
First of all, you have to have a plan.

165
00:16:05,660 --> 00:16:09,040
And I really don't think it's a plan unless it's written down.

166
00:16:09,540 --> 00:16:12,140
So it's a good idea to make a few notes.

167
00:16:12,860 --> 00:16:18,420
And in those notes, you can have the gear required for instructing the particular thing that you're going to do.

168
00:16:18,860 --> 00:16:20,260
Make sure that you're all set.

169
00:16:20,260 --> 00:16:32,480
And then once you've mastered some of the methods that we've talked about, you should be able to prepare any instructional thing for scouts in minutes.

170
00:16:33,220 --> 00:16:37,200
And you should be able to deal with the subject.

171
00:16:37,340 --> 00:16:42,240
Even if you're not fully versed in it, you know, that's a good way to begin the instructional method.

172
00:16:42,360 --> 00:16:45,819
Hey, fellas, we're all going to learn this together because I really don't know what I'm talking about.

173
00:16:45,820 --> 00:16:49,840
So why don't we start by discovering this together?

174
00:16:52,400 --> 00:17:00,360
You know, if you tend towards being long-winded and rambling as I do, plan is so incredibly important.

175
00:17:00,900 --> 00:17:06,760
And as I mentioned before, I think one aid to keeping me focused is to hand one of my scouts a stopwatch

176
00:17:06,760 --> 00:17:12,280
and tell them I have exactly two minutes or three minutes or whatever to talk.

177
00:17:12,280 --> 00:17:15,619
And they enjoy having the control.

178
00:17:16,040 --> 00:17:20,220
And they also know that I am going to talk longer than the two or three minutes.

179
00:17:20,960 --> 00:17:26,000
And they are rapturous when they're able to cut me off and hold me to the commitment.

180
00:17:27,619 --> 00:17:31,160
So that's something to think about.

181
00:17:31,700 --> 00:17:35,160
Now, who is supposed to be instructing in a scout troop?

182
00:17:35,160 --> 00:17:45,580
Well, you would think it's the guy with the master in the name, scout master or assistant scout master,

183
00:17:45,800 --> 00:17:47,880
that we should be there instructing.

184
00:17:48,000 --> 00:17:49,460
Well, I'm going to argue no.

185
00:17:49,700 --> 00:17:52,680
I'm going to argue that your goal should be not to instruct at all.

186
00:17:53,080 --> 00:18:00,120
Your goal and your job as a scout master or an assistant scout master is to train your scouts in instruction.

187
00:18:00,120 --> 00:18:06,120
Train your senior patrol leader, patrol leaders, and your older scouts in how to instruct.

188
00:18:06,500 --> 00:18:13,120
And then they will train the younger scouts in these things, not so much intentionally as by osmosis.

189
00:18:13,719 --> 00:18:19,659
When younger scouts see what the instructional methods are, hey, we stand in a circle.

190
00:18:20,120 --> 00:18:22,560
Hey, we ask a lot of questions.

191
00:18:22,979 --> 00:18:24,120
We have a round robin.

192
00:18:24,179 --> 00:18:25,520
We do this Kim's game thing.

193
00:18:25,639 --> 00:18:27,239
We use the coach and the pupil method.

194
00:18:27,240 --> 00:18:29,960
Then they will just adapt them.

195
00:18:30,500 --> 00:18:32,040
They will just adapt them naturally.

196
00:18:32,460 --> 00:18:39,620
I haven't really instructed my scouts in a specific skill or anything like that in a very long time.

197
00:18:40,160 --> 00:18:47,540
I would say on average I get to do this about once a year in an actual troop meeting or at a campout.

198
00:18:48,620 --> 00:18:52,480
Maybe on a campout, maybe let's say twice a year.

199
00:18:53,140 --> 00:18:55,960
And I do this with the permission of the senior patrol leader.

200
00:18:55,960 --> 00:19:01,100
And I ask a lot because I really enjoy instructing and I'm pretty good at it.

201
00:19:01,180 --> 00:19:04,140
I am the best instructor in my troop.

202
00:19:04,720 --> 00:19:09,400
And I don't think I'm being egotistical about that.

203
00:19:09,500 --> 00:19:10,540
I'm just telling you the truth.

204
00:19:10,720 --> 00:19:12,140
I've had a lot of practice at it.

205
00:19:12,340 --> 00:19:13,100
I'm the best guy.

206
00:19:13,260 --> 00:19:14,060
I'm the best one.

207
00:19:14,520 --> 00:19:16,560
I'm certainly better than my senior patrol leader.

208
00:19:16,940 --> 00:19:18,820
I'm certainly better than any of my patrol leaders.

209
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:20,500
I'm a great instructor.

210
00:19:20,500 --> 00:19:32,200
But if I do all the instructing, I'm denying them the opportunity to learn how to do it and to have the experience of being the instructor, which is one of the things that I really enjoy.

211
00:19:32,500 --> 00:19:40,640
So I have to kind of say, well, I don't get to instruct and I need to empower my youth leadership to do that.

212
00:19:40,640 --> 00:19:45,600
If there is something that a youth leader can do, let them do it.

213
00:19:46,180 --> 00:19:49,340
If there's something that they can't do, train them how to do it.

214
00:19:49,520 --> 00:19:53,660
The more responsibility you give to them, the better they will perform.

